🎯 Common Interview Questions & Short Answers
1️⃣ Why did you use asynchronous crawling instead of sequential requests?

To significantly reduce total crawling time. Asynchronous requests allow multiple product pages to be fetched in parallel instead of waiting for each request to finish one by one.

2️⃣ How did you control the number of concurrent requests?

I limited concurrency using an async semaphore so the crawler doesn’t overload the target website or cause excessive timeouts.

3️⃣ Why did you deduplicate product IDs before crawling?

To avoid crawling the same product multiple times, reduce unnecessary network calls, and improve overall pipeline efficiency.

4️⃣ How do you handle failures or partial errors during crawling?

Successful and failed requests are logged separately. Failed product IDs can be retried in a follow-up run without reprocessing successful ones.

5️⃣ Why did you parse HTML instead of using an API?

The product information was embedded in the page HTML as JSON, and there was no public API available, so parsing the HTML was the most reliable approach.

6️⃣ What happens if the HTML structure changes?

The crawler logs parsing failures, making it easy to detect issues. Failed items can be reviewed and retried after updating the parsing logic.

7️⃣ How does this pipeline scale with more products?

The asynchronous design allows the pipeline to scale efficiently by increasing concurrency within safe limits, without changing the overall architecture.

8️⃣ How is this pipeline used downstream?

The output is stored as structured CSV files and later ingested into the data warehouse, where it is joined with transactional data for analytics and reporting.









